<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Vermeer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a>–<a href="/years/1675">1675</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Vermeer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> painter who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>. He painted slowly, and only about 35 works are attributed to him, mostly quiet domestic interiors lit by daylight, such as The Milkmaid and Girl with a Pearl Earring, as well as the townscape View of Delft. Little known after his death, he was rediscovered in the 19th century and is now among the most admired Dutch artists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-vermeer.jpg" alt="Johannes Vermeer" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Vermeer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cropped_version_of_Jan_Vermeer_van_Delft_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vermeer was baptised in the Reformed Church on 31 October 1632. His mother, Digna Baltens, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. His father, Reijnier Janszoon, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaver from Antwerp who moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, began dealing in paintings and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> of Saint Luke as an art dealer in 1631. In 1641 he bought a larger inn on the market square. When Reijnier died in October 1652, Vermeer took over the family art business. In April 1653 he married Catharina Bolnes, a Catholic. Her mother, Maria Thins, was at first opposed, and it was probably she who insisted that he convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. The couple lived in her house on the Oude Langendijk for the rest of his life, and Catharina gave birth to 15 children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>It is unclear where Vermeer trained. Some have proposed Carel Fabritius, but there is no hard evidence, and others suggest Abraham Bloemaert. He became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> of Saint Luke on 29 December 1653 and was elected its head in 1662, then again in 1663, 1670 and 1671, which shows that his colleagues regarded him as an established craftsman. He worked slowly, probably producing about three paintings a year, and he also dealt in art. His patrons for much of his career were Pieter van Ruijven and his wife Maria de Knuijt, who bought much of his output. That kept him afloat but limited the spread of his fame.</p><p>Vermeer used the expensive pigment ultramarine, made from lapis lazuli, more lavishly and earlier than any other artist of the century, often as an underpaint beneath earth colours. He worked in domestic interiors, usually lit from the left, and the same rooms, furniture and women appear in many of his pictures. In 1672, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rampjaar">Rampjaar</a>, French troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and forces from Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. The art market collapsed, and a painting sold that year was Vermeer's last sale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vermeer died on 15 December 1675, aged 43, after a short illness, and was buried the same day in the Old Church. His widow told her creditors that the burden of his debts and unsold stock had brought on his death. She had children to raise and asked the High Court for relief from debt, and the microscopist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek">Antonie van Leeuwenhoek</a> was named trustee of the estate.</p><p>Vermeer was little known outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>. After his death he fell into obscurity, was barely mentioned in Arnold Houbraken's survey of 1718 and was omitted from later surveys for nearly two centuries. In the 19th century Gustav Friedrich Waagen and Théophile Thoré-Bürger rediscovered him. Thoré-Bürger called him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sphinx">Sphinx</a> of Delft, because so little was known of his life, and attributed 66 works to him, of which 34 are universally accepted today.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johannes Vermeer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The painting of the Dutch Golden Age, by Rembrandt van Rijn, <b>Johannes Vermeer</b>, Frans Hals and many others, developed landscape, still life and scenes of everyday life as independent genres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch Republic, with about 1.5 million people, became a great power in the 17th century and controlled a worldwide network of trade; its relative religious toleration drew refugees, merchants and thinkers, and its Golden Age produced Rembrandt, <b>Johannes Vermeer</b>, Hugo Grotius, Christiaan Huygens and Antonie van Leeuwenhoek.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Catholics tended to keep to their own section of each town: the painter <b>Johannes Vermeer</b> lived in the “Papist corner” of Delft.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delft">Delft</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.01,4.36&amp;year=1659" aria-label="Delft on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johannes Vermeer"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5229 5673h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5229 5673h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.01,4.36&amp;year=1659">What was here in 1659? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
